    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d457">

                <pb facs="0545" n="1299" xml:id="pg1299"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72111Sp1/109</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The American Chargé</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Wilson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Spanish Minister
                        of State</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">De Alhucemas</hi>)<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d457-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 109–A</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Madrid</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-11-21">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">November 21, 1917</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Excellency</hi>: On October 26 last, the French
                    Ambassador transmitted to Your Excellency’s predecessor, the Marqués de Lema, a note in
                    regard to a suggestion which he had previously made concerning the signals made
                    by the German merchant ships <hi rendition="#italic">Belgrano</hi> and <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Fangsturm</hi>. This suggestion was to the effect that
                    the only means of preventing the vessels above mentioned, and other German ships
                    in Spanish ports, from giving assistance to German submarines in their
                    operations against Allied and Spanish ships was to cause their crews to be
                    interned in the interior of Spain.</p>
                <p>I am to-day in receipt of a telegram from my Government, stating that it agrees
                    with the views expressed by the French Government through its Ambassador in the
                    above-mentioned note of October 26 last.</p>
                <p>My Government has reasons for believing that these German interned ships are used
                    as a channel of communication with German submarines, and that by means of
                    signals they give information concerning arrivals, departures, and whereabouts
                    of Spanish and Allied ships, many of which are attacked and sunk.</p>
                <p>My Government feels that these German ships, by using Spanish ports as bases of
                    intelligence for submarine operations against vessels of nations with which His
                    Majesty’s Government is at peace, are endangering the neutrality of that
                    Government, and I am accordingly instructed to express the hope that His
                    Majesty’s Government will accept the suggestion of the French Government, and
                    intern in the interior of the country the crews of German merchant ships now in
                    Spanish ports.</p>
                <p>I avail [etc.]</p>
                <closer>[No signature indicated]</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
